  "vulnerabilities": [
    {
      "type": "Unauthenticated / spoofable reporting due to missing validation of exit-request delivery",
      "severity": "high",
      "vulnerable_function": "verifyValidatorExitDelay (also affects verifyHistoricalValidatorExitDelay)",
      "location": "ValidatorExitDelayVerifier.verifyValidatorExitDelay",
      "explanation": "The contract assumes that the provided `exitRequests` blob corresponds to an actually-delivered Validators Exit Bus (VEB) batch, but it never enforces it. `_getExitRequestDeliveryTimestamp()` computes `exitRequestsHash` from user-supplied calldata and queries `veb.getDeliveryTimestamp(hash)`. If the VEB returns `0` for unknown/undelivered hashes (common pattern), the verifier still proceeds and computes `eligibleToExitInSec` using `deliveredTimestamp = 0`, effectively treating the validator as eligible based only on `earliestPossibleVoluntaryExitTimestamp`.\n\nBecause `unpackExitRequest()` is called on the user-supplied `exitRequests.data` (not on a value fetched from VEB storage), an attacker can fabricate arbitrary `(pubkey, nodeOpId, moduleId, valIndex)` pairs in calldata, provide a valid SSZ proof that the validator at `valIndex` has `exitEpoch == FAR_FUTURE_EPOCH`, and then force `stakingRouter.reportValidatorExitDelay(...)` to be called with attacker-chosen `moduleId/nodeOpId` even though no VEB exit request was ever delivered for that validator.\n\nImpact depends on the StakingRouter implementation, but if it trusts this verifier, this enables false delay reports/penalties/accounting corruption for arbitrary node operators/validators.",
      "attack_scenario": "1) Pick any active validator that has not exited yet (so `exitEpoch == FAR_FUTURE_EPOCH` in a recent beacon state).\n2) Craft `exitRequests.data` containing a single fake exit request encoding that validator’s `pubkey` and `valIndex`, but with attacker-chosen `moduleId` and `nodeOpId`. Choose any `dataFormat` that `unpackExitRequest` accepts.\n3) Ensure the fabricated `(data,dataFormat)` hash has no delivery record in VEB so `getDeliveryTimestamp(hash)` returns `0`.\n4) Build a valid `ProvableBeaconBlockHeader` for a supported slot and a `ValidatorWitness` SSZ Merkle proof for the validator leaf at `valIndex` (with correct fields from state, and `exitEpoch` implied FAR_FUTURE by this contract).\n5) Call `verifyValidatorExitDelay(...)`.\n6) The contract passes proof verification, computes a positive `eligibleToExitInSec`, and calls `stakingRouter.reportValidatorExitDelay(moduleId,nodeOpId,...)`, recording a delay for a validator that never had a delivered VEB exit request, potentially penalizing/misaccounting the targeted node operator/module.",
      "suggested_fix": "Enforce that the provided `exitRequests` batch is actually delivered/known by VEB before reporting:\n- Require a non-zero delivery timestamp, e.g. `if (deliveredTimestamp == 0) revert RootNotFound()/custom error;` (use a dedicated `ExitRequestsNotDelivered()` error).\n- Additionally, if VEB has a way to verify existence (e.g., `isDelivered(hash)`), use it.\n- Stronger: don’t trust calldata for decoding exit requests; instead have VEB expose a function to retrieve the exit request fields by `(hash,index)` from its own stored data, and use that canonical data for `(pubkey,nodeOpId,moduleId,valIndex)`.\n\nApply the same validation in both `verifyValidatorExitDelay` and `verifyHistoricalValidatorExitDelay`."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "The core SSZ/EIP-4788 proof checks look structurally sound, but the contract fails to ensure that the submitted `exitRequests` blob corresponds to a delivered/recognized VEB batch. If the VEB returns `0` for unknown hashes, attackers can fabricate exit request calldata and still pass validator state proofs, causing the verifier to call `stakingRouter.reportValidatorExitDelay` with attacker-chosen module/operator identifiers for validators that never had an exit request delivered. This can lead to false delay reporting and downstream penalties/accounting corruption if StakingRouter trusts these reports."
}
